 think: each column has size h_c. Sunny lines can cover at most k points in that column (since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column). Horizontal and slope -1 lines can also cover at most one point per column each. So to cover h_c points, we need at least h_c - (k + number_of_non_sunny_non_vertical_lines_in_that_column) vertical lines. But we can have at most one horizontal line per column (since horizontal line covers all columns at same y coordinate). Actually horizontal line y = const covers one point per column (if that y coordinate appears in that column). Similarly slope -1 line x+y = const covers at most one point per column (since each column has at most one point with a given sum). So each horizontal or slope -1 line can contribute at most one point per column. So the total number of non-vertical lines that can contribute to a column is at most (n-k) (the total number of non-sunny lines). But they may be used across columns.\n\nBut the solution's argument is simpler: For any column, at most k points can be covered by sunny lines. So if column size > k, we need at least one vertical line to cover the rest. So any column with size > k must have a vertical line. Since column sizes are decreasing from n to 1, the columns with size > k are those with index c ≤ n+1 - (k+1) = n - k. Actually size h_c = n+1-c. So h_c > k => n+1-c > k => c n+1 - k => c ≤ n - k. So columns 1 through n-k must have vertical lines. So we need at least n-k vertical lines. But we have only n-k non-sunny lines total. So all non-sunny lines must be vertical lines. So we cannot have any horizontal or slope -1 lines. So all non-sunny lines are vertical. So we have exactly n-k vertical lines covering columns 1 through n-k. That leaves columns n-k+1 through n (the rightmost k columns) to be covered by sunny lines only (since no non-vertical non-sunny lines). But each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so to cover all points in those columns, we need at least the maximum column size among those columns. Let's examine.\n\nColumns n-k+1 through n have sizes: h_{n-k+1} = n+1-(n-k+1) = k, h_{n-k+2} = k-1, ..., h_n = 1. So the total number of points in those columns is sum_{i=1}^{k} i = k(k+1)/2. We have k sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column, so each sunny line can cover at most k points (one per each of the k columns). So total capacity of sunny lines is at most k*k = k^2 points. Since k(k+1)/2 ≤ k^2 for k≥1 (true), capacity is enough. But we also need to ensure that each column's points are covered. Since each column has size i (for i from 1 to k), we need i distinct sunny lines to cover column n-k+i (size i). So we need at least i sunny lines for each column i. Since we have k sunny lines, we can satisfy all columns because i ≤ k. So capacity wise it's okay. So the counting argument that k≥4 impossible may be flawed? Let's examine more carefully.\n\nThe solution's argument for k≥4: \"Since a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having k≥4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most n-4 vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.\" This seems to be a specific argument using the three rightmost columns. Let's examine.\n\nIf k≥4, we have at least 4 sunny lines. The three rightmost columns are columns n, n-1, n-2 with sizes 1,2,3. For k≥4, we have at least 4 sunny lines, so we could cover those columns with sunny lines. But the solution says \"the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\". Wait, earlier they argued that if k≤2, column n-2 (size 3) > k, so need vertical line. But if k≥4, column n-2 size 3 ≤ k, so we could cover column n-2 with sunny lines. So the argument that column n-2 forces a vertical line only holds for k≤2. So the solution's claim that for k≥4 we also need a vertical line for column n-2 is not correct. Let's examine the solution's text:\n\nIn Section 3: \"Since a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having k≥4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most n-4 vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered. Hence k≥4 is also impossible.\"\n\nBut earlier they argued that for k≤2, column n-2 forces a vertical line. For k≥4, they claim the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2. That seems contradictory. Actually for k≥4, column n-2 size 3 ≤ k, so we could cover its three points with three sunny lines (one per point). So we don't need a vertical line. So the argument is flawed. However, maybe they rely on the fact that we have only n total lines, and we need to allocate vertical lines to columns left of n-2. Let's examine more systematically.\n\nWe have n lines total, k sunny lines, n-k non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. Horizontal and slope -1 lines can each cover at most one point per column, but they can cover points across columns. However, they cannot cover more than one point per column. So they are not more powerful than sunny lines in terms of covering multiple points in a column. So the only way to cover a column with many points is vertical lines. So we need vertical lines for columns where the number of points exceeds the number of non-vertical lines that can contribute to that column (sunny + horizontal + slope -1). Since we have k sunny lines, and at most (n-k) non-sunny lines, but among those non-sunny lines, only vertical lines can cover multiple points in a column. Horizontal and slope -1 lines each can cover at most one point per column. So the t